          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            What is called the “BRST complex” in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            literature is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+infinity-algebroid"} qDGCA
            which is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chevalley-Eilenberg+algebra"} Chevalley-Eilenberg algebra
            of the
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+infinity-algebroid"} algebroid
            which is the differential version in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+theory"} Lie theory
            of the
            = succeed "-groupoid" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %ul
            %li
              %p whose space of objects is the space of configurations/histories of a given physical system;
            %li
              %p whose morphisms describe the gauge transformations between these configurations/histories;
            %li
              %p
                whose
                = succeed "-morphisms" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
                = succeed "-fold" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
          %p The generators of the BRST complex are called
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                in degree 0:
                = succeed ";" do
                  %strong fields
            %li
              %p
                in degree 1:
                = succeed ";" do
                  %strong
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ghost+field"} ghost field
            %li
              %p
                in degree 2:
                = succeed ";" do
                  %strong
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ghost-of-ghost+fields"} ghost-of-ghost fields
            %li
              %p etc.
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cochain+cohomology"} cochain cohomology
            of the BRST complex is called, of course,
            = succeed "." do
              %em BRST cohomology
          %h2#details Details
          %p For details see at
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A+first+idea+of+quantum+field+theory"} A first idea of quantum field theory
              the chapter
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a{:href => "A+first+idea+of+quantum+field+theory#Gauge+symmetries"} Gauge symmetries
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %p
            The BRST complex described a homotopical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient"} quotient
            of a space by an infinitesimal
            = succeed "." do
